/** Standalone batch T -- new 4-case standard (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress).
 * All solvers below were independently written and verified byte-for-byte against each problem's
 * real official Sample before being trusted to generate boundary/tricky/stress expected output.
 *
 * uva-1193-radar-installation: classic interval-covering greedy. Each island (x,y) with |y|<=d maps
 *   to a coverable x-interval [x-sqrt(d^2-y^2), x+sqrt(d^2-y^2)]; sort by RIGHT endpoint and greedily
 *   place a radar at each newly-uncovered interval's right end. Tricky case is the classic trap: a
 *   sort-by-LEFT-endpoint greedy would place the same number of radars here by coincidence in many
 *   inputs, so the tricky case is constructed so only right-endpoint-sort greedy gives the true
 *   minimum (2), catching that specific wrong algorithm.
 * uva-11934-magic-formula: brute force f(x) for x in [0,L], L<1000, so O(L) per test case is required
 *   to stay fast; tricky case exercises negative a/b/c requiring correct negative-modulo normalization.
 * uva-11942-lumberjack-sequencing: strict monotonic (ascending or descending) check over each line of
 *   10 distinct ints; tricky case includes a zigzag sequence that a "check only first two elements"
 *   bug would misjudge as Ordered.
 * uva-11956-brainfuck: interpret a subset of brainfuck (>,<,+,-,.) over 100 circular memory cells,
 *   each byte circular mod 256; tricky case exercises pointer wraparound (< from cell 0) and byte
 *   wraparound in both directions (- past 0, + past 255).
 * uva-11957-checkers: DP paths from the white piece's start to the top row (row 0), moving diagonally
 *   forward or jumping over a black piece (board state is static -- pieces are never actually removed,
 *   per the problem statement), counted mod 1000007. Tricky case includes a jump that would land off
 *   the board edge (must be blocked, not wrap) and a board where two distinct move sequences converge
 *   on the same cell (tests correct modular accumulation).
 * uva-11960-divisor-game: sieve divisor counts up to 1e6, then a running "record holder" (largest N'
 *   achieving the highest divisor count seen so far, ties broken toward the larger N' per the verified
 *   sample behavior) queried per test. Tricky case probes values immediately around several classic
 *   highly-composite numbers (12, 24, 60, 120, 840).
 * uva-11987-almost-union-find: weighted DSU with a "move p to q's set" operation, implemented via
 *   virtual re-parenting: moving p subtracts it from its current root's (size,sum), allocates a fresh
 *   singleton virtual node for p, and unions that node into q's set, with real[p] repointed to the new
 *   node -- this sidesteps DSU's inability to natively "split" a node out of its tree. Tricky case is
 *   the problem statement's OWN worked example (n=5) plus repeated no-op unions/moves and a
 *   move-the-same-element-twice-in-a-row sequence, verifying real[] repointing stays correct across
 *   repeated moves.
 */
